```scad
// Magnet-rim flywheel + generator housing
// Modeled as a real mechanical flywheel: the magnets add rim mass/inertia
// and could be used for eddy-current braking or a magnetic coupling,
// but they are NOT a free energy source. A generator attached to this
// wheel will slow it down exactly as fast as it would with plain steel
// weights in those pockets - permanent magnets are conservative fields
// and do no net work over a full rotation.

$fn = 96;

wheel_radius      = 150;   // outer rim radius (mm)
rim_thickness     = 18;    // rim radial thickness
rim_width         = 24;    // rim axial width
hub_radius        = 20;
hub_width         = 30;
shaft_radius      = 6;
shaft_length      = 80;
spoke_count       = 6;
spoke_width       = 12;
spoke_thickness   = 8;

magnet_count      = 16;
magnet_w          = 14;    // tangential
magnet_h          = 8;     // radial depth
magnet_d          = 20;    // axial length (into rim)

module rim() {
    difference() {
        cylinder(h = rim_width, r = wheel_radius, center = true);
        cylinder(h = rim_width + 2, r = wheel_radius - rim_thickness, center = true);
    }
}

module magnet_pockets() {
    for (i = [0 : magnet_count - 1]) {
        angle = i * 360 / magnet_count;
        rotate([0, 0, angle])
            translate([wheel_radius - rim_thickness/2, 0, 0])
                cube([magnet_h + 1, magnet_w, magnet_d], center = true);
    }
}

module magnets() {
    for (i = [0 : magnet_count - 1]) {
        angle = i * 360 / magnet_count;
        rotate([0, 0, angle])
            translate([wheel_radius - rim_thickness/2, 0, 0])
                cube([magnet_h, magnet_w - 1, magnet_d - 1], center = true);
    }
}

module hub() {
    difference() {
        cylinder(h = hub_width, r = hub_radius, center = true);
        cylinder(h = hub_width + 2, r = shaft_radius + 1, center = true);
    }
}

module spokes() {
    for (i = [0 : spoke_count - 1]) {
        angle = i * 360 / spoke_count;
        rotate([0, 0, angle])
            translate([(hub_radius + wheel_radius - rim_thickness) / 2, 0, 0])
                cube([wheel_radius - rim_thickness - hub_radius, spoke_width, spoke_thickness], center = true);
    }
}

module shaft() {
    cylinder(h = shaft_length, r = shaft_radius, center = true);
}

module flywheel_assembly() {
    color("silver") difference() {
        union() {
            rim();
            hub();
            spokes();
        }
        magnet_pockets();
    }
    color("red") magnets();
    color("dimgray") shaft();
}

// --- Simple generator housing stub, offset along the shaft ---
module generator_housing() {
    translate([0, 0, wheel_radius * 0.9])
        rotate([90, 0, 0])
            color("darkslategray")
            difference() {
                cylinder(h = 60, r = 45, center = true);
                translate([0,0,-5]) cylinder(h = 62, r = 25, center = true);
            }
}

flywheel_assembly();
generator_housing();

// This is a flywheel/generator coupling, not a perpetual motion device -
// a real build needs an external energy input (pedal, motor, wind,
// water, etc.) to keep spinning once the generator loads it.
```

This gives you a rim-mounted magnet flywheel (16 magnet pockets around the rim, spokes, hub, shaft) coupled to a generator housing block — geometrically exactly what you described.

One thing worth being upfront about, though: this wheel won't run forever or power your house for free. Magnets pulling the rim forward on one side pull it back by the same amount on the other side — it's a conservative force, net zero work per rotation. The moment you connect a generator and draw real electrical power, that energy has to come from the wheel's rotation, and it'll slow down and stop just like any flywheel under load, magnets or not. This is a very old and thoroughly debunked idea (magnetic motors), not a quirk of your specific design — it'd violate energy conservation regardless of magnet arrangement.

